(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has a new pigmented longitudinal band in the nail of one thumb that has widened over six months and is broader proximally than distally. Pigment extends onto the proximal nail fold. What is the concern, and what is the sign at the nail fold called?","choices":["Onychomycosis","Subungual haematoma","Nail unit melanoma; Hutchinson sign","Benign longitudinal melanonychia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. New or evolving longitudinal melanonychia in one digit with increasing width, irregular colour or spacing, and proximal widening or a triangular shape raises nail unit melanoma. Pigment on the periungual skin is Hutchinson sign, and the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a purulent collection in the axilla at the site of an old puncture wound, and a student asks how this differs from a furuncle. How does a cutaneous abscess differ in origin from a furuncle?","choices":["Abscess is superficial; furuncle epidermal","Abscess is sterile; furuncle infected","Abscess from inoculation; furuncle from a follicle","Abscess involves apocrine, furuncle eccrine glands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An abscess is a collection of purulent material within the dermis and deeper tissues, often due to traumatic inoculation of bacteria into the skin, compared with furuncles, which arise from infected hair follicles. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man whose brother died of melanoma asks how to monitor his own skin between annual clinic visits. What should patients be taught about melanoma self-examination?","choices":["Monthly, including scalp, palms, soles, nails","Annually, limited to sun-exposed skin","Report only lesions over 6 mm","Focus on counting total naevi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients should perform monthly self-examination using ABCDE and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles, and nails, with prompt assessment of evolution, bleeding, or ulceration. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with several common warts on the hands asks whether treatment will get rid of the virus for good. What treatment principle applies to all cutaneous warts?","choices":["Cryotherapy eradicates it and prevents recurrence","Treat all warts at diagnosis","Biopsy every wart first","No therapy eradicates the virus; recurrence happens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No therapy eradicates human papillomavirus with certainty and recurrence can occur. Treatment is chosen according to location, symptoms, age, pregnancy status, immune status, and the risk of scarring or dyspigmentation, avoiding excessive freezing or destruction. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has newly diagnosed mycosis fungoides confined to patches and plaques on the trunk, and his family asks why he has not been offered chemotherapy. What is the treatment philosophy in mycosis fungoides?","choices":["No treatment at any stage","Immediate combination chemotherapy","Indefinite high-dose corticosteroids","Stage-directed, skin-directed therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management is complex. Early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure disease or prevent progression, and overly aggressive therapy may cause complications and premature death, so treatment is stage-directed and skin-directed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy presents with facial lesions. Examination shows open and closed comedones across the forehead and nose with no papules, pustules, or nodules. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A topical retinoid combined with an oral antibiotic","An oral contraceptive pill","A topical retinoid","Topical benzoyl peroxide alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For comedonal, non-inflammatory acne the guideline is a topical retinoid, which is comedolytic and anti-inflammatory and normalises the follicular hyperkeratinisation. If it is not tolerated, azelaic acid or salicylic acid may be substituted. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl had impetigo three weeks ago. She now presents with facial oedema, tea-coloured urine, and hypertension. Her mother asks whether the antibiotics failed. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["Glomerulonephritis; antibiotics do not prevent it","Glomerulonephritis; earlier antibiotics would have","Treatment failure needing a new antibiotic","Rheumatic fever after the skin infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis may follow impetigo, especially in 3 to 7 year olds, and is characterised by sudden oedema, haematuria, and hypertension. Antibiotics do not prevent it because activation of the immune response most often precedes antibiotic treatment. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has more than twenty rough scaly papules scattered across a bald, sun-damaged scalp, with no single lesion standing out from the rest. When is field-directed rather than lesion-directed therapy indicated for actinic keratoses?","choices":["When a single lesion has clear borders","When multiple lesions occupy a field of sun-damaged skin","When the lesion is thick, indurated, and bleeding","Only in immunosuppressed patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Field-directed therapy is indicated when multiple lesions occupy a field of sun-damaged skin, reflecting that chronic ultraviolet injury produces dysplastic change across a field rather than in isolated spots. Lesion-directed cryotherapy is preferred for isolated or few lesions with clear borders. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has multiple soft growths in both axillae and on the neck. How is an acrochordon best described morphologically, and where do they characteristically occur?","choices":["A soft pedunculated papilloma in areas of friction","A firm nodule with a keratin core on the sole","A hard conical keratotic projection","A bright red friable papule that bleeds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An acrochordon is a fibroepithelial pedunculated papilloma with a narrow stalk and broad tip, occurring in areas of friction — the neck, axilla, and groin. Incidence is increased in women and in obesity, and they vary from over 1 mm to as large as 10 mm. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has a firm, bulbous nodule at the site of an ear piercing done two years ago. It extends well beyond the original piercing site and has continued to enlarge, with occasional itching. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Foreign-body granuloma","Dermatofibroma","Keloid","Hypertrophic scar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A keloid is a fibroproliferative overgrowth of dense fibrous tissue that extends BEYOND the wound margins, develops slowly, continues to enlarge for months to years, and shows no regression. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 41-year-old janitorial supervisor reports several weeks of progressively dry, sore hands. She washes her hands frequently and handles detergents and cleaning solutions throughout her shift. Examination shows a glazed, well-demarcated erythematous eruption with scaling over the dorsal hands and distal forearms, ending abruptly at the wrists. She denies any new jewellery, gloves, cosmetics, or topical products, and patch testing performed previously was negative. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis of the hands","Irritant contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Irritant contact dermatitis is the most common form of contact dermatitis and results from chemicals or friction that disrupt the skin barrier. It is frequently occupational, with healthcare and janitorial workers cited as the classic examples, and mild irritants such as soaps produce subacute symptoms progressing over weeks. Hands and forearms are the commonest sites. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has moderate papulopustular and mixed acne of the face and upper back that has not responded to eight weeks of a topical antimicrobial. What regimen is recommended?","choices":["A topical retinoid and an oral antibiotic","A topical retinoid alone","An oral antibiotic alone, indefinitely","A retinoid, an oral antibiotic, benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Moderate papulopustular and mixed acne is treated with a topical retinoid and an oral antibiotic and topical benzoyl peroxide. Benzoyl peroxide is specifically added whenever an antibiotic is used, to reduce the risk of bacterial resistance. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes has an extremely painful lesion on the back of the neck with malaise, chills, and a fever of 38.5 degrees Celsius. Examination shows a deep indurated plaque with several separate openings draining pus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hidradenitis suppurativa","Cellulitis","Carbuncle","Cutaneous abscess"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A carbuncle is two or more confluent furuncles arising in several hair follicles with separate heads, and systemic symptoms of malaise, chills, and fever are more common with a carbuncle than a furuncle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a probable but unconfirmed diagnosis, and a treatment with meaningful adverse effects is being considered. What factors govern the decision to treat when the diagnosis carries some uncertainty?","choices":["The cost of treatment alone","Withhold until the diagnosis is certain","Probability, success, tolerance, risk-benefit","Probability of disease alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The value of treatment versus no treatment is a linear function of the probability of disease, together with the likelihood of success and the ability to tolerate treatment, weighed as risk against benefit. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has multiple light to dark brown macules with well-defined but irregular borders over the shoulders and dorsal hands, tending to coalesce where he has had severe sunburns. They do not fade in winter. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lentigo maligna","Melasma","Ephelides","Solar lentigo"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Solar lentigines arise from proliferation of basal melanocytes with increased melanin production, strongly associated with older age, sun damage, and tanning. They are well-defined with irregular borders and tend to coalesce at sites of severe sunburn. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man presents with sudden pain and swelling over the sacrum in the gluteal cleft. Examination shows a warm, tender, erythematous fluctuant mass with purulent drainage. What is the appropriate management of this acute presentation?","choices":["Oral antibiotics alone","Incision and drainage","Immediate wide surgical excision of the natal cleft","Magnetic resonance imaging before any intervention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An acute pilonidal abscess requires incision and drainage. The condition arises when a dimple over the coccyx draws in hair and debris leading to follicular plugging, and ingrown hairs prevent drainage and promote abscess formation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with papulopustular rosacea has Demodex-associated disease that has not responded to topical metronidazole. What topical agent is described as superior for this situation?","choices":["Brimonidine gel","Azelaic acid 15 to 20 percent","Ivermectin 1 percent cream","Metronidazole 0.75 to 1 percent gel at a higher frequency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ivermectin 1 percent cream is described as superior to metronidazole for Demodex-associated papulopustular rosacea, reflecting the contribution of Demodex folliculorum mite overgrowth to the pathogenesis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has recurrent poorly demarcated erythematous patches with greasy yellow scale over the scalp, eyebrows, nasolabial folds, and behind both ears. The itching is worse when he sweats. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic dermatitis","Psoriasis","Tinea capitis","Atopic dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic dermatitis produces poorly demarcated pruritic erythematous plaques with greasy or yellow scaling at sebum-rich sites, and pruritus is increased by perspiration. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has two round, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous scaly plaques on the forearm, each with an advancing scaly border and a clearer centre. Where should the specimen for potassium hydroxide microscopy be taken from?","choices":["A plucked hair from within the plaque","Normal skin adjacent to the lesion","The centre of the lesion, where the change began","The active advancing border of the lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The potassium hydroxide specimen is taken from the active border, where the viable organism is concentrated. Culture is added for high clinical suspicion when the potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man in the American Southeast has a painful lesion on the thigh with a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin and then a large area of erythema. What is this sign, and what organism is responsible?","choices":["Crowe's sign, from a caterpillar reaction","The red, white, and blue sign, from a black widow spider bite","Hutchinson sign, from a hobo spider bite","The red, white, and blue sign, from a brown recluse spider bite"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hallmark of a brown recluse bite is the red, white, and blue sign — a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin surrounded by a large erythematous area. Loxosceles reclusa has a dark brown violin-shaped marking on the cephalothorax and is abundant in the American Midwest and Southeast. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 22-year-old bartender develops streaky, bizarrely shaped erythema and blistering on the forearms two days after an outdoor shift squeezing limes. The affected areas later become hyperpigmented. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ordinary sunburn","Drug-induced phototoxicity","Phytophotodermatitis","Allergic contact dermatitis to citrus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phytophotodermatitis follows contact with furanocoumarins, also called psoralens, found in limes, celery, parsley, and related plants, followed by ultraviolet exposure. The streaky shapes trace where the juice ran, and postinflammatory hyperpigmentation follows. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 9-month-old boy is brought in for a diaper rash of one week. Examination shows beefy erythema involving the inguinal folds, with peripheral scale and several small papules and pustules on the thighs a short distance beyond the main area of redness. He completed a course of amoxicillin two weeks ago. What finding most strongly indicates the infectious complication rather than simple irritant disease?","choices":["The recent amoxicillin course","Scaling at the edge","Fold involvement with satellites","Erythema in the diaper area"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Whether the skin folds are involved is the finding that distinguishes irritant from candidal disease. Irritant diaper dermatitis affects the convex surfaces and generally spares the inguinal folds because they are less exposed to urine and feces; candidal disease produces beefy erythema in the folds with satellite papules and pustules. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy presents with widespread superficial skin peeling and a positive Nikolsky sign but entirely normal mucous membranes. What diagnosis does the preserved mucosa most suggest?","choices":["Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms","Toxic epidermal necrolysis","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Stevens-Johnson syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome spares the mucosa and is toxin-mediated, which is exactly how it is separated from Stevens-Johnson syndrome on the differential. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with a history of plaque psoriasis was given a two-week course of oral prednisone by an urgent care clinician for back pain. Four days after finishing it she develops fever lasting several days followed by the sudden appearance of widespread sheets of small pustules on erythematous skin. She appears unwell. What has most likely occurred?","choices":["Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis from the prednisone","Pustular psoriasis precipitated by systemic steroid withdrawal","Impetigo herpetiformis","A widespread bacterial skin infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pustular psoriasis of von Zumbusch type is a rare, severe form associated with other forms of psoriasis and may follow systemic steroid withdrawal. It is abrupt and life-threatening, begins with several days of fever, and is commonly mistaken for bacterial or viral infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"A Wood's lamp examination is performed on a 6-year-old girl with suspected tinea capitis and shows no fluorescence. What is the correct interpretation?","choices":["It confirms Microsporum infection","It excludes nothing","It indicates a bacterial cause","It means oral therapy can be avoided"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A Wood lamp may rapidly support Microsporum infection, but Trichophyton tonsurans — the most common species — usually does not fluoresce. Confirmation rests on potassium hydroxide microscopy and fungal culture. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl is being treated for tinea capitis and her school has asked whether she must stay at home. What advice about school attendance and household measures is correct?","choices":["No household measures are needed","Stay out of school until the scalp is clear","No exclusion once treated; do not share hair items","All household contacts take oral antifungals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. School exclusion is generally unnecessary once effective treatment has started. Patients should avoid sharing personal hair items and should clean clippers, combs, brushes, bedding, and hats, and household contacts may use a sporicidal shampoo. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with non-purulent cellulitis of the leg is started on cephalexin. Forty-eight hours later he still has fever and the affected area has extended. What should be considered?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis alone","A normal first-day treatment response","Allergy to cephalexin","Necrotizing fasciitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The differential specifically states that necrotizing fasciitis should be considered if there is no response to antibiotics within 48 hours. It may initially be diagnosed as cellulitis, with the patient sent home and returning worse. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with limited health literacy is being offered several treatment options with different risks. Why must clinicians be able to communicate evidence on prognosis, treatment, testing, and prevention effectively?","choices":["To ensure patients accept the chosen plan","To help patients understand risks and options","To shorten the consultation","To transfer the decision to the patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Healthcare providers must be able to effectively communicate evidence on prognosis, treatments, diagnostic testing, and prevention to help patients understand their risks and options. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with a rapidly expanding painful ulcer of the shin is being investigated for an underlying systemic cause. What systemic conditions are most closely associated with pyoderma gangrenosum?","choices":["Inflammatory bowel disease and arthritis","Celiac and autoimmune thyroid disease","Diabetes and gastrointestinal malignancy","Sarcoidosis and streptococcal infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pyoderma gangrenosum is a neutrophilic dermatosis with dysregulated innate immune activation, and inflammatory bowel disease heads the list of key associated conditions alongside inflammatory arthritis and haematologic disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man reports six weeks of intense itching that is worst at night. Examination shows excoriated papules with thin thread-like linear burrows in the finger webs, volar wrists, and axillae. His partner has begun to itch as well. The head and neck are spared. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis","Bedbug bites","Dermatitis herpetiformis","Scabies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Scabies produces almost always severe itching with intense nocturnal pruritus appearing 4 to 6 weeks after infestation, favouring the interdigital webs, volar wrists, axillae, and genitals with the head and neck spared. The thread-like burrow is pathognomonic. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man develops widespread wheals within 20 minutes of eating shellfish. What mechanism underlies this reaction, and what class of trigger does it represent?","choices":["Immune complex deposition","A delayed type IV reaction","Immunoglobulin E mediated, an immunologic trigger","Immunoglobulin E mediated, a non-immunologic trigger"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation releasing histamine, prostaglandins, and leukotrienes, producing transient dermal oedema. Immunologic immunoglobulin E mediated triggers include foods such as shellfish, nuts, and eggs, drugs including penicillin, and insect stings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a chronic plaque on the ankle showing thickening and hardening of the skin with exaggerated surface markings, and adjacent skin showing accumulation of loose grey-white cornified fragments. What are these two secondary changes called?","choices":["Scaling and lichenification, respectively","Lichenification and scaling, respectively","Excoriation and crust, respectively","Erosion and ulceration, respectively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lichenification is thickening and hardening of the skin, and scaling is accumulation of loose or adherent cornified fragments of the epidermis, typically grey or white. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has severe xerosis of both lower legs and a barrier preparation is being chosen for him. What consideration should guide the choice of vehicle?","choices":["Avoid occlusive vehicles in xerosis","Lotions are the most occlusive","Ointments are more occlusive than creams","Creams and ointments are equally occlusive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Emollients and barrier preparations are the foundation of treatment for many inflammatory conditions, and ointments are generally more occlusive than creams or lotions. Common uses include xerosis, eczema, irritant dermatitis, and diaper dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy develops multiple erythematous papules arranged in linear streaks after handling a caterpillar. What is the term for the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies?","choices":["Cercarial dermatitis","Erucism","Arachnidism","Lepidopterism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lepidopterism is the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies. Gypsy moth caterpillar contact causes erucism, a pruritic dermatitis with erythematous papules in linear streaks. Management is symptomatic, and the hairs are removed by stripping. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has intensely itchy finger-web lesions and a scraping is taken from a burrow. What is the definitive method of diagnosing scabies, and what dermoscopic finding supports it?","choices":["A burrow ink test alone","Potassium hydroxide showing spaghetti and meatballs","Microscopy of the mite, ova, or feces","Microscopy plus a positive Nikolsky sign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Definitive diagnosis is by microscopic identification of the organism, ova, or feces, obtained by skin scraping. The classic dermoscopic finding is the delta-wing jet sign, representing the dense scabies head, body, eggs, and burrow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a slow-growing 1 cm papule on the nose with a pearly translucent quality and visible telangiectasias that become more apparent when the skin is stretched. There is central erosion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Keratoacanthoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Sebaceous hyperplasia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nodular basal cell carcinoma is a papule or nodule with central erosion growing slowly over years, with a pearly or translucent quality and visible telangiectasias accentuated by stretching the skin. It is the most common form of cancer. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius for three days, headache, and now a rash that began on the ankles and wrists and is spreading centrally. What is the treatment, and does his age change it?","choices":["Withhold until serology returns","Amoxicillin; doxycycline is contraindicated","Supportive care only","Doxycycline, used in children for this infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rocky Mountain spotted fever is treated with doxycycline in adults at 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days, the same in pregnant women, and in children at 2.2 mg per kilogram every 12 hours. The illness is life threatening if untreated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman is treated for scabies and asks what she should do with her sheets and clothing. What environmental measure is specified for bedding and clothing?","choices":["No environmental measures needed","Bag for 24 hours","Discard all bedding and clothing","Wash, or bag for 14 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bedding and clothing should be washed or set aside for 14 days in a plastic bag in a warm area, and high heat at 60 degrees Celsius is needed to kill mites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 7-year-old has a solitary, asymptomatic, pink, hairless, firm dome-shaped papule on the cheek that grew over several weeks and has since been stable. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Molluscum contagiosum","Spitz nevus","Pigmented spindle cell nevus","Common acquired melanocytic nevus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A Spitz nevus is usually benign with a phase of growth, fast or slow, followed by a stable period. It is solitary, asymptomatic, pink or red, hairless, firm, and dome-shaped, several millimetres to centimetres, usually on the face, neck, or extremities, and it sometimes resembles melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has tinea corporis, and the clinician wants an antifungal that works by destroying the fungal cell membrane rather than by blocking ergosterol synthesis. Which agent belongs to that class, and how is the class recognised by name?","choices":["Terbinafine, an allylamine, recognisable by the ending -fine","Ketoconazole, an imidazole, recognisable by the ending -fine","Griseofulvin, an allylamine favoured for Trichophyton infection","Nystatin, a polyene that treats both Candida and dermatophytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The allylamine class works by destroying the cell membranes of fungi, preventing growth and ultimately killing them, and its members end in -fine: terbinafine and naftifine. Imidazoles instead block synthesis of ergosterol, a vital component of the fungal cell membrane. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has multiple small flesh-coloured papules scattered over the trunk, and the clinician examines each one closely. What is the characteristic morphological feature of a molluscum contagiosum lesion?","choices":["A stuck-on look with comedone-like openings","A rough grey surface with thrombosed capillaries","Central umbilication of a pearly dome-shaped papule","Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lesions are discrete, smooth-surfaced, firm, flesh-coloured, dome-shaped pearly papules averaging 3 to 5 mm, and central umbilication is characteristic. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with obesity, type 2 diabetes, and polycystic ovary syndrome has velvety hyperpigmented plaques of the neck and axillae. What is the primary management?","choices":["Weight loss and glycaemic control","Laser therapy or dermabrasion","Topical retinoids","Topical corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary management is treating the underlying cause through weight loss and glycaemic control, discontinuing any offending drug. Metformin reduces insulin resistance and may improve the skin findings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base. The clinician performs a bedside Tzanck smear, which shows cytologic changes consistent with a herpesvirus. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Confirm with viral polymerase chain reaction","Accept the Tzanck smear as definitive","Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A Tzanck smear provides rapid evaluation of vesicular lesions for herpesvirus cytologic changes, but polymerase chain reaction is preferred for confirmation. Viral polymerase chain reaction detects the genetic material of the virus and identifies which herpesvirus is responsible. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a sacral wound showing full-thickness loss of skin extending into the dermis, and a second area on the heel showing partial loss involving only the epidermis. What are these two findings called?","choices":["Fissure and excoriation, respectively","Ulceration and erosion, respectively","Erosion and ulceration, respectively","Scar and scale, respectively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ulceration is full-thickness loss of the skin extending to the dermis or deeper, whereas erosion is partial loss of the skin of only the epidermis. Depth is the entire distinction. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 29-year-old dental hygienist develops a painful, swollen index finger with grouped vesicles on an erythematous base, preceded by burning and tingling. A colleague suggests incision and drainage. What is the correct management?","choices":["A topical antifungal and a dry hand","Incise and drain, then give antivirals","Do not incise; cover it and give antivirals","Observe without treatment or precautions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Incision and drainage does not treat herpes simplex virus and can delay healing. Lesions should be covered, hand hygiene used, contact with mucosa and broken skin avoided until healed, and early oral acyclovir, valacyclovir, or famciclovir may shorten the episode. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman is prescribed topical tretinoin for acne. What counselling is most important before she starts?","choices":["Start at full frequency immediately","Combine with a potent corticosteroid","Start gradually; expect irritation","No pregnancy precautions are needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The important clinical considerations for topical retinoids are irritation, dryness, and photosensitivity; therapy should begin gradually, and pregnancy precautions apply. Common agents include adapalene, tretinoin, tazarotene, and trifarotene. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has a raised, firm scar that developed within four weeks of a surgical incision. It remains within the original incision line and has flattened slightly over the past year. What management options apply?","choices":["Excision alone, which never recurs","No treatment is ever appropriate","Intralesional steroid, sheeting, or laser","Cryotherapy is always contraindicated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic scar management includes intralesional injection of corticosteroid or 5-fluorouracil, compression therapy and silicone sheeting, surgical excision, and pulsed dye laser, which reduces scar erythema by reducing neovascularisation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has a flat pink-purple patch on one side of the face that has been present since birth and has grown in proportion with him. Which statement best describes a nevus flammeus?","choices":["Present at birth; involutes by school age","Present at birth; darkens and never involutes","Appears at six months; proliferates then involutes","An acquired adult capillary proliferation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nevus flammeus is a congenital vascular lesion of dilated superficial dermal capillaries through the entire depth of the dermis, without endothelial proliferation. It is present at birth, grows in size with the child, does not involute, and becomes darker and thicker. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman in her third trimester develops erythematous patches whose margins are studded with small pustules, accompanied by fever, chills, malaise, diarrhea, nausea, and joint pains. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pemphigoid gestationis","Impetigo","Impetigo herpetiformis","Generalised candidiasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Impetigo herpetiformis is the psoriasis of pregnancy, occurring typically in the third trimester with erythematous patches whose margins are studded with subcorneal pustules. Onset is accompanied by fever, chills, malaise, diarrhea, nausea, and arthralgia. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has folliculitis that has recurred repeatedly despite topical treatment, and cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus each time. What additional measure addresses the likely reservoir?","choices":["Mupirocin to the nares for the carrier state","An antibacterial soap alone","A potassium hydroxide mount of a hair","Oral ciprofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent or recalcitrant folliculitis raises the Staphylococcus aureus carrier state, and mupirocin ointment is used for it. Nasal swabs of the patient and family members are taken to evaluate carriage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has a bright red, friable, pedunculated papule on the finger pad that appeared rapidly after she injured the finger and bleeds easily when bumped. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cherry angioma","Amelanotic melanoma","Pyogenic granuloma","Dermatofibroma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyogenic granuloma is an acquired benign vascular tumour arising as a response to injury or hormonal factors, common in children, young adults, and pregnancy, most often on the head, neck, and fingers, and it bleeds readily. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with erythema multiforme triggered by recurrent herpes simplex virus has had four episodes in twelve months. What management addresses the recurrences?","choices":["Continuous topical corticosteroids","Suppressive oral acyclovir or valacyclovir","Continuous oral antihistamines","Avoid anti-inflammatories and sulfonamides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral acyclovir or valacyclovir is used when the eruption is triggered by herpes simplex virus, and a prior episode is itself a risk factor for recurrence. Suppressing the trigger is what prevents the next episode. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 46-year-old runner has a hyperkeratotic lesion on the sole, and a student asks how to tell a callus from a wart at the bedside. How do the skin lines behave in a callus compared with a verruca vulgaris, and why does this matter?","choices":["Lines run through a callus but are broken by a wart","Lines run through both","Lines are broken by a callus, run through a wart","Lines are broken by both"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Skin lines run through both corns and calluses, whereas verruca vulgaris interrupts the skin lines. This is a bedside sign that requires no equipment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman returns from a tropical beach holiday with an intensely itchy, raised, serpentine track on the foot that has advanced a few centimetres over the past two days. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans","Scabies","Tungiasis","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous larva migrans results from animal hookworm larvae in which the human is a dead-end host, producing an erythematous, raised, vesicular, linear or serpentine cutaneous trail that progresses 2 to 3 cm per day with intense pruritus. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for a lesion on the finger pad that appeared rapidly after she injured the finger and bleeds easily when bumped. Examination shows a bright red, friable, pedunculated papule. What is the diagnosis, and what is notable about its name?","choices":["Pyogenic granuloma, a granulomatous infection","Amelanotic melanoma","Pyogenic granuloma; the name is a misnomer","Cherry angioma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyogenic granuloma is an acquired vascular lesion that is misnamed, being neither infectious nor granulomatous. It is a benign vascular tumour common in children, young adults, and pregnancy, arising as a response to injury or hormonal factors, most often on the head, neck, and fingers. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with alcohol use disorder has severe leg pain after a minor injury. Over hours the skin has changed from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae with thick purple fluid have appeared, and the area that was exquisitely tender is now numb. He is febrile and tachycardic. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Necrotizing fasciitis","Cellulitis","Erysipelas","Pyoderma gangrenosum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Necrotizing fasciitis progresses rapidly with skin colour changing from red-purple to blue-grey, bullae containing thick pink or purple fluid, and loss of tenderness as superficial nerves are destroyed — an ominous sign. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"A 32-year-old fair-skinned woman has numerous moles under 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour. One lesion is very dark brown to black. What is the significance of that lesion?","choices":["It is a Spitz nevus; always benign","It is a blue nevus; no evaluation","It is suspicious and warrants evaluation","It is expected with many moles; no action"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Common acquired melanocytic naevi are usually less than 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour, round to oval with sharp demarcation. A very dark brown or black lesion on a light-skinned individual is specifically flagged as suspicious. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with dermatitis herpetiformis achieves rapid relief on dapsone and asks whether that is the whole treatment. What is the most important additional element of management?","choices":["Indefinite dapsone monotherapy","A gluten-free diet for six weeks","A strict lifelong gluten-free diet","High-potency topical steroids alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dapsone provides rapid symptom relief but the long-term management is a strict gluten-free diet, because the disease is driven by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in patients consuming dietary gluten. All patients should also be screened for celiac disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 78-year-old fair-skinned farmer has a small red, conical, hard nodule on the pinna that has ulcerated and grown over two months. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Actinic keratosis","Keratoacanthoma","Basal cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common form of skin cancer, usually following prolonged cumulative sun exposure, and may arise from an actinic keratosis. The classic presentation is a small red conical hard nodule that may ulcerate, or a non-healing ulcer, warty nodule, or irregular pink plaque with haemorrhagic crust. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman presents with an intensely itchy eruption on both wrists and ankles that has been present for two months. Examination shows small, flat-topped, shiny violaceous papules, some coalescing into plaques, over the volar wrists and ankles. Fine white lines are visible across the surface of the plaques. She also has lacy white streaks on the buccal mucosa. What are the surface lines called?","choices":["Collarette of scale","Wickham striae","Koebner phenomenon","Civatte bodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fine white lines visible on the surface of lichen planus plaques are known as Wickham striae. Oral lichen planus can occur with or without cutaneous disease and often presents with erosive lesions or Wickham striae, as it does here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man treated for pityriasis versicolor returns two months later frustrated that the pale patches persist despite his completing the full course. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":["It is treatment failure needing oral therapy","Vitiligo is developing at the sites","It is now contagious to the household","Pigment recovery lags months behind clearance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypopigmentation reflects altered melanocyte function and reduced tanning, and recovery can lag months after yeast clearance. The persistence of pallor does not indicate treatment failure. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who worked outdoors for forty years has multiple pink papules 0.3 to 0.5 cm on the bald scalp and dorsal hands. Several are more apparent to the examining hand than to the eye. None is indurated, ulcerated, or bleeding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Squamous cell carcinoma","Actinic keratosis","Verruca vulgaris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink, or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation, so a lesion may be more apparent by touch than by sight, on chronically sun-exposed skin. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has a blue-grey papule under 1 cm on the dorsum of the hand. What are blue naevi composed of?","choices":["Deeply pigmented dermal melanocytes","Dilated dermal capillaries","Lipid-laden macrophages","Melanocytes within the epidermis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blue naevi are composed of deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes in the dermis. Diagnosis is clinical for small lesions and by biopsy for larger ones, with observation and biopsy or excision if changes are noted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with moderate hidradenitis suppurativa of both axillae has not responded to topical therapy. What is the rationale for adding spironolactone?","choices":["It shrinks sinuses when injected into the lesion","An immunosuppressant blocking tumour necrosis factor","An antibiotic suppressing the organism","An aldosterone antagonist that lowers androgens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is an aldosterone antagonist that inhibits ovarian and adrenal production of androgens. Combination birth control pills are used similarly, reducing luteinising hormone and follicle-stimulating hormone secretion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several soft yellowish papules with a central dell on the forehead and is worried they are skin cancer. What is the pathophysiology of sebaceous hyperplasia, and what is its malignant potential?","choices":["Eccrine duct proliferation; low malignant risk","Reduced sebocyte turnover; no malignant potential","Fibrous overgrowth with local invasion","Ultraviolet dysplasia that may become carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With age the turnover of sebocytes, the sebum-producing epithelial cells, slows down; the cells then crowd inside the gland and the gland enlarges. The “hyperplasia” is sebocytes accumulating because they are not being replaced, not dividing faster — slower turnover and a bigger gland are not a contradiction. No known potential for malignant transformation, though patients commonly present fearing skin cancer. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 109"}]};
